(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("platform-tools",{"platform-tools/backstage-software-catalog-and-templates":{"html":"\u003cp>A portal is only as good as its data. Backstage gives a place to describe who owns what and a way to start new services the right way, but it needs care to stay accurate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Entities: components, systems, APIs and owners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>catalog-info.yaml files and discovery\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Software templates and scaffolding\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Plugins, TechDocs and search\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running Backstage as a product\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"entities\">Entities\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The catalog models components such as services and libraries, systems that group them, APIs they provide or consume, resources such as databases and the owners of each. Relationships give answers to common questions: who owns this, what depends on it, which API does it expose.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"metadata-files\">Metadata files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each repository holds a \u003ccode>catalog-info.yaml\u003c/code> that describes the component. The catalog discovers these files from the source control provider and refreshes them. Because the file lives with the code, owners can update it in the same pull request, and a validation check can fail the build when it is missing or invalid.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"templates\">Templates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A software template asks a few questions, then creates a repository, a pipeline, a deployment configuration and a catalog entry in one run. Templates encode the golden path. Keep them small, version them and track which services were created from which version so they can be updated.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plugins-and-docs\">Plugins and docs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Plugins add views for CI results, Kubernetes status, cost or on call. TechDocs renders documentation from markdown in the repository. Add plugins when a real team asks for them, because each one is code to maintain and upgrade.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"running-as-a-product\">Running as a product\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Backstage is a framework you build and run, not a product you install. Assign a team, track adoption and catalog completeness, and fix stale ownership first. A portal full of unowned and outdated entries loses trust quickly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"repo&gt;cat;cat&gt;tpl;tpl&gt;ui\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Backstage: software catalog and templates\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"repo\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Repository\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cat\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Software catalog\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tpl\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Templates\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ui\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developer portal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Repository to Software catalog; 2, Software catalog to Templates; 3, Templates to Developer portal.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"repo\">\u003cdt>Repository \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> catalog-info.yaml.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing file.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cat\">\u003cdt>Software catalog \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Entities and owners.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tpl\">\u003cdt>Templates \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Golden paths.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Old versions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ui\">\u003cdt>Developer portal \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One place.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Low trust.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-catalog-full-of-unowned-services\">Incident: A catalog full of unowned services\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During an outage nobody could find who owned the failing service in the portal.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"yq '.spec.owner' catalog-info.yaml\necho &quot;owner group exists: no&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>yq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.spec.owner&#x27;\u003c/span> catalog-info.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">group:default/team-that-left\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner group exists: no&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Teams had been reorganised and ownership in many files pointed to groups that no longer existed, so the page for the failing service listed nobody. The platform team added a check that every entity owner resolves to a real group, a weekly report of orphaned services and a rule that reorganisations update ownership files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Entities with no valid owner\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Metadata kept outside the repository\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Too many plugins too early\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Templates that are never updated\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No adoption or completeness metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"entities","text":"Entities"},{"id":"metadata-files","text":"Metadata files"},{"id":"templates","text":"Templates"},{"id":"plugins-and-docs","text":"Plugins and docs"},{"id":"running-as-a-product","text":"Running as a product"},{"id":"incident-a-catalog-full-of-unowned-services","text":"Incident: A catalog full of unowned services"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Backstage","u":"https://github.com/backstage/backstage"},{"t":"Backstage documentation","u":"https://backstage.io/docs/"},{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://github.com/cncf/tag-app-delivery/blob/main/platforms-whitepaper/v1/paper.md"}],"text":"A portal is only as good as its data. Backstage gives a place to describe who owns what and a way to start new services the right way, but it needs care to stay accurate. What you will learn Entities: components, systems, APIs and owners catalog-info.yaml files and discovery Software templates and scaffolding Plugins, TechDocs and search Running Backstage as a product Entities The catalog models components such as services and libraries, systems that group them, APIs they provide or consume, resources such as databases and the owners of each. Relationships give answers to common questions: who owns this, what depends on it, which API does it expose. Metadata files Each repository holds a catalog-info.yaml that describes the component. The catalog discovers these files from the source control provider and refreshes them. Because the file lives with the code, owners can update it in the same pull request, and a validation check can fail the build when it is missing or invalid. Templates A software template asks a few questions, then creates a repository, a pipeline, a deployment configuration and a catalog entry in one run. Templates encode the golden path. Keep them small, version them and track which services were created from which version so they can be updated. Plugins and docs Plugins add views for CI results, Kubernetes status, cost or on call. TechDocs renders documentation from markdown in the repository. Add plugins when a real team asks for them, because each one is code to maintain and upgrade. Running as a product Backstage is a framework you build and run, not a product you install. Assign a team, track adoption and catalog completeness, and fix stale ownership first. A portal full of unowned and outdated entries loses trust quickly. Backstage: software catalog and templates Flow in order: 1, Repository to Software catalog; 2, Software catalog to Templates; 3, Templates to Developer portal. Outside party Data store Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Repository Outside party What it does: catalog-info.yaml. If it fails or is weak: Missing file. Software catalog Data store What it does: Entities and owners. If it fails or is weak: Stale. Templates Step What it does: Golden paths. If it fails or is weak: Old versions. Developer portal Service What it does: One place. If it fails or is weak: Low trust. Incident: A catalog full of unowned services Incident During an outage nobody could find who owned the failing service in the portal. $ yq &#x27;.spec.owner&#x27; catalog-info.yaml group:default/team-that-left $ echo \"owner group exists: no\" (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Teams had been reorganised and ownership in many files pointed to groups that no longer existed, so the page for the failing service listed nobody. The platform team added a check that every entity owner resolves to a real group, a weekly report of orphaned services and a rule that reorganisations update ownership files. Common mistakes Entities with no valid owner Metadata kept outside the repository Too many plugins too early Templates that are never updated No adoption or completeness metrics"},"platform-tools/crossplane-compositions-and-self-service-infrastructure":{"html":"\u003cp>Crossplane lets a platform team publish a simple API such as Database and keep the cloud details behind it. Developers ask for what they need, and the platform decides how it is built.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Providers and managed resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Composite resource definitions and compositions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Claims: the developer facing API\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reconciliation, drift and deletion policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Operating Crossplane and choosing it over Terraform\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"providers\">Providers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A provider is a controller that manages resources in an external system, such as AWS, Azure or Kubernetes. Each external resource appears as a managed resource object in the cluster. The controller creates it, watches it and keeps it matching the spec, which also corrects drift.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compositions\">Compositions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A composite resource definition declares a new API with a schema, for example a PostgreSQLInstance with size and region fields. A composition maps that API to concrete managed resources: a database instance, a subnet group, a secret. The platform team owns the composition and can change what runs behind the API.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"claims\">Claims\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Developers create a claim in their namespace. Crossplane builds the composite resource and the managed resources behind it, and writes connection details to a secret in the same namespace. The developer never needs cloud credentials or knowledge of the underlying resources.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drift-and-deletion\">Drift and deletion\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Controllers keep reconciling, so manual changes in the cloud console are reverted or reported. Set the deletion policy deliberately. With Delete the cloud resource is removed when the object is deleted, and with Orphan it is kept. For databases use protective settings, backups and review before deletion.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operations\">Operations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pin provider versions, test compositions with sample claims and render them before release, monitor reconcile errors and API rate limits, and limit what the provider credentials can do. Choose Crossplane when you want a Kubernetes native API with continuous reconciliation, and Terraform when plan and apply workflows suit the team better.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"dev&gt;xrd;xrd&gt;comp;comp&gt;prov\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Crossplane: compositions and self service infrastructure\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developer claim\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"xrd\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Composite API\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"comp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Composition\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prov\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Provider and cloud\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Developer claim to Composite API; 2, Composite API to Composition; 3, Composition to Provider and cloud.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>Developer claim \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Simple request.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong size.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"xrd\">\u003cdt>Composite API \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Schema.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Breaking change.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"comp\">\u003cdt>Composition \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Maps to resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Untested.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prov\">\u003cdt>Provider and cloud \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Credentials.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-deleted-claim-that-removed-a-production-database\">Incident: A deleted claim that removed a production database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A developer deleted a namespace and the database behind the claim was deleted with it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get postgresqlinstance orders-db -n shop -o jsonpath=&quot;{.spec.deletionPolicy}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get postgresqlinstance orders-db -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{.spec.deletionPolicy}&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Delete\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The composition used the default Delete policy and had no backup or protection. When the namespace was removed, the claim, the composite resource and the cloud database all went with it. The platform team changed the production composition to Orphan with final snapshots, added a policy that blocks namespace deletion when claims exist, and documented the choice in the API.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Default deletion policy for production data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing a composition without tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unpinned provider versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Provider credentials with broad access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exposing every cloud setting in the claim\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},{"id":"compositions","text":"Compositions"},{"id":"claims","text":"Claims"},{"id":"drift-and-deletion","text":"Drift and deletion"},{"id":"operations","text":"Operations"},{"id":"incident-a-deleted-claim-that-removed-a-production-database","text":"Incident: A deleted claim that removed a production database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Crossplane documentation","u":"https://docs.crossplane.io/"},{"t":"Kubernetes documentation","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/"},{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://github.com/cncf/tag-app-delivery/blob/main/platforms-whitepaper/v1/paper.md"}],"text":"Crossplane lets a platform team publish a simple API such as Database and keep the cloud details behind it. Developers ask for what they need, and the platform decides how it is built. What you will learn Providers and managed resources Composite resource definitions and compositions Claims: the developer facing API Reconciliation, drift and deletion policy Operating Crossplane and choosing it over Terraform Providers A provider is a controller that manages resources in an external system, such as AWS, Azure or Kubernetes. Each external resource appears as a managed resource object in the cluster. The controller creates it, watches it and keeps it matching the spec, which also corrects drift. Compositions A composite resource definition declares a new API with a schema, for example a PostgreSQLInstance with size and region fields. A composition maps that API to concrete managed resources: a database instance, a subnet group, a secret. The platform team owns the composition and can change what runs behind the API. Claims Developers create a claim in their namespace. Crossplane builds the composite resource and the managed resources behind it, and writes connection details to a secret in the same namespace. The developer never needs cloud credentials or knowledge of the underlying resources. Drift and deletion Controllers keep reconciling, so manual changes in the cloud console are reverted or reported. Set the deletion policy deliberately. With Delete the cloud resource is removed when the object is deleted, and with Orphan it is kept. For databases use protective settings, backups and review before deletion. Operations Pin provider versions, test compositions with sample claims and render them before release, monitor reconcile errors and API rate limits, and limit what the provider credentials can do. Choose Crossplane when you want a Kubernetes native API with continuous reconciliation, and Terraform when plan and apply workflows suit the team better. Crossplane: compositions and self service infrastructure Flow in order: 1, Developer claim to Composite API; 2, Composite API to Composition; 3, Composition to Provider and cloud. Outside party Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Developer claim Outside party What it does: Simple request. If it fails or is weak: Wrong size. Composite API Step What it does: Schema. If it fails or is weak: Breaking change. Composition Step What it does: Maps to resources. If it fails or is weak: Untested. Provider and cloud Service What it does: Creates resources. If it fails or is weak: Credentials. Incident: A deleted claim that removed a production database Incident A developer deleted a namespace and the database behind the claim was deleted with it. $ kubectl get postgresqlinstance orders-db -n shop -o jsonpath= \"{.spec.deletionPolicy}\" Delete (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The composition used the default Delete policy and had no backup or protection. When the namespace was removed, the claim, the composite resource and the cloud database all went with it. The platform team changed the production composition to Orphan with final snapshots, added a policy that blocks namespace deletion when claims exist, and documented the choice in the API. Common mistakes Default deletion policy for production data Changing a composition without tests Unpinned provider versions Provider credentials with broad access Exposing every cloud setting in the claim"},"platform-tools/argo-cd-applicationsets-and-fleet-delivery":{"html":"\u003cp>One Argo CD application per service per cluster does not scale. ApplicationSets generate them from a list, a folder structure or a set of clusters, so a hundred clusters are not a hundred hand written files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The hub and spoke model for many clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>ApplicationSet generators: list, git and cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sync waves, hooks and ordering\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Progressive rollout across the fleet\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Access, secrets and failure handling\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hub-and-spoke\">Hub and spoke\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A central Argo CD instance, or a few of them, manages applications in many target clusters registered as destinations. This gives one place to see the state of the fleet. Consider the blast radius: a failed central instance stops deployments everywhere, so plan its availability and keep clusters able to run without it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"generators\">Generators\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An ApplicationSet uses a template and generators to create applications. A list generator takes explicit values, a git generator creates one application per directory or file in a repository, and a cluster generator creates one per registered cluster that matches labels. A matrix generator combines two, for example every service on every production cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ordering\">Ordering\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Sync waves order resources inside an application, for example namespaces and CRDs before the workloads that use them. Hooks run jobs before or after a sync, such as a database migration. Keep waves few and simple, because complex ordering is hard to debug.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"progressive-rollout\">Progressive rollout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not push a change to every cluster at once. Use rolling sync strategies in ApplicationSets or separate labels for rings: a canary cluster, then a small group, then the rest, with health checks between steps. Pause the rollout automatically when an application becomes degraded.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-and-secrets\">Access and secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Give teams access to their own projects and destinations through AppProjects and RBAC, and restrict which repositories and clusters each project can use. Keep secrets out of manifests by using an external secrets operator or sealed secrets. Alert on out of sync and degraded applications.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"git&gt;as;as&gt;hub;hub&gt;cl\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Argo CD ApplicationSets and fleet delivery\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"git\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Config repository\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"as\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">ApplicationSet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"hub\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Argo CD hub\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cluster fleet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Config repository to ApplicationSet; 2, ApplicationSet to Argo CD hub; 3, Argo CD hub to Cluster fleet.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"git\">\u003cdt>Config repository \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Folders per env.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong merge.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"as\">\u003cdt>ApplicationSet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Generators.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Over broad.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"hub\">\u003cdt>Argo CD hub \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Syncs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Single point.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cl\">\u003cdt>Cluster fleet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rings.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> All at once.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-bad-change-that-reached-every-cluster-in-minutes\">Incident: A bad change that reached every cluster in minutes\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A broken ingress setting was merged and an ApplicationSet synced it to forty clusters at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app list -o name | wc -l\nargocd app list --sync-status OutOfSync -o name | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app list -o name | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">120\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app list --sync-status OutOfSync -o name | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">40\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The ApplicationSet used a cluster generator for all production clusters with automated sync, so one merge changed everything at once. The team added rollout rings by cluster label, a canary ring with health gates, manual approval for the final ring, and an automatic pause when applications became degraded.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Syncing every cluster at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One broad AppProject for everyone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets stored in manifests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Complex sync wave chains\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No plan for the hub being unavailable\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"hub-and-spoke","text":"Hub and spoke"},{"id":"generators","text":"Generators"},{"id":"ordering","text":"Ordering"},{"id":"progressive-rollout","text":"Progressive rollout"},{"id":"access-and-secrets","text":"Access and secrets"},{"id":"incident-a-bad-change-that-reached-every-cluster-in-minutes","text":"Incident: A bad change that reached every cluster in minutes"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/"},{"t":"Kubernetes documentation","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/"},{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://github.com/cncf/tag-app-delivery/blob/main/platforms-whitepaper/v1/paper.md"}],"text":"One Argo CD application per service per cluster does not scale. ApplicationSets generate them from a list, a folder structure or a set of clusters, so a hundred clusters are not a hundred hand written files. What you will learn The hub and spoke model for many clusters ApplicationSet generators: list, git and cluster Sync waves, hooks and ordering Progressive rollout across the fleet Access, secrets and failure handling Hub and spoke A central Argo CD instance, or a few of them, manages applications in many target clusters registered as destinations. This gives one place to see the state of the fleet. Consider the blast radius: a failed central instance stops deployments everywhere, so plan its availability and keep clusters able to run without it. Generators An ApplicationSet uses a template and generators to create applications. A list generator takes explicit values, a git generator creates one application per directory or file in a repository, and a cluster generator creates one per registered cluster that matches labels. A matrix generator combines two, for example every service on every production cluster. Ordering Sync waves order resources inside an application, for example namespaces and CRDs before the workloads that use them. Hooks run jobs before or after a sync, such as a database migration. Keep waves few and simple, because complex ordering is hard to debug. Progressive rollout Do not push a change to every cluster at once. Use rolling sync strategies in ApplicationSets or separate labels for rings: a canary cluster, then a small group, then the rest, with health checks between steps. Pause the rollout automatically when an application becomes degraded. Access and secrets Give teams access to their own projects and destinations through AppProjects and RBAC, and restrict which repositories and clusters each project can use. Keep secrets out of manifests by using an external secrets operator or sealed secrets. Alert on out of sync and degraded applications. Argo CD ApplicationSets and fleet delivery Flow in order: 1, Config repository to ApplicationSet; 2, ApplicationSet to Argo CD hub; 3, Argo CD hub to Cluster fleet. Data store Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Config repository Data store What it does: Folders per env. If it fails or is weak: Wrong merge. ApplicationSet Step What it does: Generators. If it fails or is weak: Over broad. Argo CD hub Service What it does: Syncs. If it fails or is weak: Single point. Cluster fleet Service What it does: Rings. If it fails or is weak: All at once. Incident: A bad change that reached every cluster in minutes Incident A broken ingress setting was merged and an ApplicationSet synced it to forty clusters at once. $ argocd app list -o name | wc -l 120 $ argocd app list --sync-status OutOfSync -o name | wc -l 40 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The ApplicationSet used a cluster generator for all production clusters with automated sync, so one merge changed everything at once. The team added rollout rings by cluster label, a canary ring with health gates, manual approval for the final ring, and an automatic pause when applications became degraded. Common mistakes Syncing every cluster at once One broad AppProject for everyone Secrets stored in manifests Complex sync wave chains No plan for the hub being unavailable"},"platform-tools/cluster-api-and-fleet-lifecycle":{"html":"\u003cp>Creating a cluster is easy. Keeping fifty of them patched, consistent and replaceable is the real work. Cluster API treats clusters themselves as Kubernetes objects.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Management cluster and workload clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Providers and machine templates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrades and rolling replacement\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cluster classes and consistent fleets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backup, recovery and day two operations\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"management-and-workload-clusters\">Management and workload clusters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A management cluster runs the Cluster API controllers. It creates and manages workload clusters on a chosen infrastructure through provider controllers for clouds or bare metal. Protect the management cluster, back it up and keep it small, because it holds the desired state of the whole fleet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"providers-and-templates\">Providers and templates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Cluster object points to a control plane object and an infrastructure object. Machine templates describe the size and image of nodes, and a MachineDeployment keeps a number of machines running. Changing the template creates new machines and replaces the old ones in a rolling way.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrades\">Upgrades\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>To upgrade, change the Kubernetes version on the control plane object, wait for it to roll, then update the machine deployments. Upgrades replace machines rather than patch them in place. Test on a canary cluster first, check workload disruption budgets and skip no more than the supported number of minor versions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cluster-classes\">Cluster classes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A ClusterClass defines a reusable cluster shape with variables. Clusters created from it stay consistent, and a change to the class can be rolled out to many clusters. This avoids a fleet where every cluster is slightly different.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"day-two\">Day two\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Plan backups of the management cluster, certificate renewal, node health checks with automatic remediation and a runbook for replacing a failed control plane member. Monitor machine and cluster conditions, not only Kubernetes metrics.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"mgmt&gt;cls;cls&gt;md;md&gt;wl\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Cluster API and fleet lifecycle\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mgmt\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Management cluster\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cls\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">ClusterClass\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"md\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Machine deployments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workload clusters\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Management cluster to ClusterClass; 2, ClusterClass to Machine deployments; 3, Machine deployments to Workload clusters.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mgmt\">\u003cdt>Management cluster \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Desired state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cls\">\u003cdt>ClusterClass \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shared shape.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Drift.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"md\">\u003cdt>Machine deployments \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rolling nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stuck.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wl\">\u003cdt>Workload clusters \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run apps.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unpatched.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-an-upgrade-that-stalled-halfway\">Incident: An upgrade that stalled halfway\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A control plane upgrade stopped after the first machine and left the cluster mixed.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get machines -n fleet -l cluster.x-k8s.io/cluster-name=prod-eu\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get machines -n fleet -l cluster.x-k8s.io/cluster-name=prod-eu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            PHASE        VERSION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">prod-eu-cp-0    Running      v1.31.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">prod-eu-cp-1    Provisioning v1.32.1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new machine could not start because the machine image for the new version was not available in the region, so the rollout waited. The team found the image error in the machine conditions, published the image, and added a pre upgrade check that confirms images and quotas exist in every region before a rollout.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A management cluster with no backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Clusters that differ from each other\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading production first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping minor versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No pre checks for images and quotas\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"management-and-workload-clusters","text":"Management and workload clusters"},{"id":"providers-and-templates","text":"Providers and templates"},{"id":"upgrades","text":"Upgrades"},{"id":"cluster-classes","text":"Cluster classes"},{"id":"day-two","text":"Day two"},{"id":"incident-an-upgrade-that-stalled-halfway","text":"Incident: An upgrade that stalled halfway"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Cluster API repository","u":"https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/cluster-api"},{"t":"Kubernetes documentation","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/"},{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://github.com/cncf/tag-app-delivery/blob/main/platforms-whitepaper/v1/paper.md"}],"text":"Creating a cluster is easy. Keeping fifty of them patched, consistent and replaceable is the real work. Cluster API treats clusters themselves as Kubernetes objects. What you will learn Management cluster and workload clusters Providers and machine templates Upgrades and rolling replacement Cluster classes and consistent fleets Backup, recovery and day two operations Management and workload clusters A management cluster runs the Cluster API controllers. It creates and manages workload clusters on a chosen infrastructure through provider controllers for clouds or bare metal. Protect the management cluster, back it up and keep it small, because it holds the desired state of the whole fleet. Providers and templates A Cluster object points to a control plane object and an infrastructure object. Machine templates describe the size and image of nodes, and a MachineDeployment keeps a number of machines running. Changing the template creates new machines and replaces the old ones in a rolling way. Upgrades To upgrade, change the Kubernetes version on the control plane object, wait for it to roll, then update the machine deployments. Upgrades replace machines rather than patch them in place. Test on a canary cluster first, check workload disruption budgets and skip no more than the supported number of minor versions. Cluster classes A ClusterClass defines a reusable cluster shape with variables. Clusters created from it stay consistent, and a change to the class can be rolled out to many clusters. This avoids a fleet where every cluster is slightly different. Day two Plan backups of the management cluster, certificate renewal, node health checks with automatic remediation and a runbook for replacing a failed control plane member. Monitor machine and cluster conditions, not only Kubernetes metrics. Cluster API and fleet lifecycle Flow in order: 1, Management cluster to ClusterClass; 2, ClusterClass to Machine deployments; 3, Machine deployments to Workload clusters. Service Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Management cluster Service What it does: Desired state. If it fails or is weak: Lost. ClusterClass Data store What it does: Shared shape. If it fails or is weak: Drift. Machine deployments Step What it does: Rolling nodes. If it fails or is weak: Stuck. Workload clusters Service What it does: Run apps. If it fails or is weak: Unpatched. Incident: An upgrade that stalled halfway Incident A control plane upgrade stopped after the first machine and left the cluster mixed. $ kubectl get machines -n fleet -l cluster.x-k8s.io/cluster-name=prod-eu NAME PHASE VERSION prod-eu-cp-0 Running v1.31.2 prod-eu-cp-1 Provisioning v1.32.1 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The new machine could not start because the machine image for the new version was not available in the region, so the rollout waited. The team found the image error in the machine conditions, published the image, and added a pre upgrade check that confirms images and quotas exist in every region before a rollout. Common mistakes A management cluster with no backup Clusters that differ from each other Upgrading production first Skipping minor versions No pre checks for images and quotas"},"platform-tools/ephemeral-environments-and-preview-deployments":{"html":"\u003cp>Shared staging is where changes wait in a queue. Ephemeral environments let each change be tested alone, then disappear, if you control cost and data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why ephemeral environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Namespace, virtual cluster or full cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data: seeds, snapshots and masked copies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cost limits and automatic cleanup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Developer experience and links in the pull request\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why\">Why\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A shared staging environment causes conflicts, stale data and waiting. A preview environment per pull request lets reviewers click a URL, tests run against clean state and bugs found do not block others. It works best when environments are cheap and automatic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"isolation-level\">Isolation level\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A namespace per environment is cheap, but cluster wide resources and CRDs are shared. A virtual cluster, for example with vcluster, gives each environment its own API server inside a host cluster, with better isolation for tests that change cluster scoped objects. A whole cluster per environment is the strongest and the most costly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"data\">Data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Environments need realistic data without real customer data. Seed from generated fixtures, restore snapshots of masked production copies or use database branching. Never copy raw production data into a place with weaker access controls.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-and-cleanup\">Cost and cleanup\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set time to live on every environment, scale to zero when idle, apply resource quotas and use cheap node pools. A pull request that closes must delete its environment. Run a reaper job that removes anything without an owner or past its time limit, and report the cost of previews.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"experience\">Experience\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Post the URL, status and logs link as a comment on the pull request. Keep creation under a few minutes by using cached images and pre warmed pools. Share one template for all teams, so a new service gets previews through the golden path.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"pr&gt;env;env&gt;data;env&gt;reap\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Ephemeral environments and preview deployments\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pr\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pull request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"env\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Preview environment\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"data\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Masked data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"reap\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reaper and quotas\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pull request to Preview environment; 2, Preview environment to Masked data; 3, Preview environment to Reaper and quotas.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pr\">\u003cdt>Pull request \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Triggers it.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Closed, not cleaned.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"env\">\u003cdt>Preview environment \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Namespace or vcluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Shared state.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"data\">\u003cdt>Masked data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Seeded.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Real data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"reap\">\u003cdt>Reaper and quotas \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cleanup.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-preview-bill-that-grew-every-week\">Incident: A preview bill that grew every week\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Preview environments from closed pull requests were never deleted and quietly used nodes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get ns -l type=preview --no-headers | wc -l\nkubectl get ns -l type=preview -o jsonpath=&quot;{.items[0].metadata.creationTimestamp}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get ns -l \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=preview --no-headers | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">312\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get ns -l \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=preview -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{.items[0].metadata.creationTimestamp}&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2026-05-02T09:14:00Z\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cleanup step ran only when a pull request merged, so abandoned and closed pull requests left their environments behind. The team added a time to live label, a reaper job that deletes environments older than three days or without an open pull request, and quotas per team with a weekly cost report.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No time to live on environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copying raw production data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cleanup only on merge\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared databases between previews\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No quotas per team\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why","text":"Why"},{"id":"isolation-level","text":"Isolation level"},{"id":"data","text":"Data"},{"id":"cost-and-cleanup","text":"Cost and cleanup"},{"id":"experience","text":"Experience"},{"id":"incident-a-preview-bill-that-grew-every-week","text":"Incident: A preview bill that grew every week"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"vcluster","u":"https://github.com/loft-sh/vcluster"},{"t":"Kubernetes documentation","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/"},{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://github.com/cncf/tag-app-delivery/blob/main/platforms-whitepaper/v1/paper.md"}],"text":"Shared staging is where changes wait in a queue. Ephemeral environments let each change be tested alone, then disappear, if you control cost and data. What you will learn Why ephemeral environments Namespace, virtual cluster or full cluster Data: seeds, snapshots and masked copies Cost limits and automatic cleanup Developer experience and links in the pull request Why A shared staging environment causes conflicts, stale data and waiting. A preview environment per pull request lets reviewers click a URL, tests run against clean state and bugs found do not block others. It works best when environments are cheap and automatic. Isolation level A namespace per environment is cheap, but cluster wide resources and CRDs are shared. A virtual cluster, for example with vcluster, gives each environment its own API server inside a host cluster, with better isolation for tests that change cluster scoped objects. A whole cluster per environment is the strongest and the most costly. Data Environments need realistic data without real customer data. Seed from generated fixtures, restore snapshots of masked production copies or use database branching. Never copy raw production data into a place with weaker access controls. Cost and cleanup Set time to live on every environment, scale to zero when idle, apply resource quotas and use cheap node pools. A pull request that closes must delete its environment. Run a reaper job that removes anything without an owner or past its time limit, and report the cost of previews. Experience Post the URL, status and logs link as a comment on the pull request. Keep creation under a few minutes by using cached images and pre warmed pools. Share one template for all teams, so a new service gets previews through the golden path. Ephemeral environments and preview deployments Flow in order: 1, Pull request to Preview environment; 2, Preview environment to Masked data; 3, Preview environment to Reaper and quotas. Outside party Service Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Pull request Outside party What it does: Triggers it. If it fails or is weak: Closed, not cleaned. Preview environment Service What it does: Namespace or vcluster. If it fails or is weak: Shared state. Masked data Data store What it does: Seeded. If it fails or is weak: Real data. Reaper and quotas Observability What it does: Cleanup. If it fails or is weak: Missing. Incident: A preview bill that grew every week Incident Preview environments from closed pull requests were never deleted and quietly used nodes. $ kubectl get ns -l type =preview --no-headers | wc -l 312 $ kubectl get ns -l type =preview -o jsonpath= \"{.items[0].metadata.creationTimestamp}\" 2026-05-02T09:14:00Z (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The cleanup step ran only when a pull request merged, so abandoned and closed pull requests left their environments behind. The team added a time to live label, a reaper job that deletes environments older than three days or without an open pull request, and quotas per team with a weekly cost report. Common mistakes No time to live on environments Copying raw production data Cleanup only on merge Shared databases between previews No quotas per team"},"platform-tools/measuring-platforms-dora-and-developer-experience":{"html":"\u003cp>A platform is a product with internal customers. Without measurement, you cannot tell whether it saves time or only adds a layer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>DORA metrics and what they show\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Developer experience: surveys and friction\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Platform adoption and time to first deploy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cost, reliability and toil measures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using metrics without gaming them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dora-metrics\">DORA metrics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate and time to restore service measure delivery performance. The first two show speed and the last two show stability. Measure them per team from the delivery system and look at trends, not single numbers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"developer-experience\">Developer experience\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Quantitative data misses friction. Run a short survey each quarter and ask about build time, waiting, confusing tools and cognitive load. Pair it with interviews and observation. Track the same questions over time to see whether changes helped.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"adoption\">Adoption\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Measure how many services use each golden path, the time from a new engineer start to first production deploy, and the time to create a new service. Low adoption is feedback about the product, not a failure of the teams that do not adopt.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-reliability-and-toil\">Cost, reliability and toil\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track platform availability and incident counts, the support load in tickets and chat, and the share of engineering time spent on repetitive operational work. Show cost per service or per team so the savings of shared services are visible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"avoiding-gaming\">Avoiding gaming\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Any metric used as a target will be gamed. Use metrics to find problems and start conversations, not to rank teams. Combine several measures, keep definitions stable and share results openly with the teams they describe.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"dora&gt;dx;dx&gt;adopt;adopt&gt;act\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Measuring platforms: DORA and developer experience\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dora\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">DORA metrics\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dx\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developer survey\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"adopt\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Adoption data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Platform roadmap\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, DORA metrics to Developer survey; 2, Developer survey to Adoption data; 3, Adoption data to Platform roadmap.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dora\">\u003cdt>DORA metrics \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Speed and stability.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Single number.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dx\">\u003cdt>Developer survey \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Friction.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not asked.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"adopt\">\u003cdt>Adoption data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Golden path use.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Low.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Platform roadmap \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Acts on data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignores it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-metric-that-rose-while-the-work-got-worse\">Incident: A metric that rose while the work got worse\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Deployment frequency was used as a target and teams split changes into tiny deploys.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -h metrics -c &quot;select team, count(*) as deploys, avg(lines_changed) as avg_lines from deploys where month = '2026-10' group by team order by deploys desc limit 2&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -h metrics -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select team, count(*) as deploys, avg(lines_changed) as avg_lines from deploys where month = &#x27;2026-10&#x27; group by team order by deploys desc limit 2&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> team   | deploys | avg_lines\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> team-a | 612     | 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>When managers ranked teams by deployment frequency, one team deployed trivial changes to raise the count while lead time and failures did not improve. The platform team stopped ranking, reported the four measures together as trends, added the survey results and used them to choose the next improvement.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using DORA as a team ranking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring only speed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never asking developers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring low adoption\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing metric definitions often\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"dora-metrics","text":"DORA metrics"},{"id":"developer-experience","text":"Developer experience"},{"id":"adoption","text":"Adoption"},{"id":"cost-reliability-and-toil","text":"Cost, reliability and toil"},{"id":"avoiding-gaming","text":"Avoiding gaming"},{"id":"incident-a-metric-that-rose-while-the-work-got-worse","text":"Incident: A metric that rose while the work got worse"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"DORA research","u":"https://dora.dev/"},{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://github.com/cncf/tag-app-delivery/blob/main/platforms-whitepaper/v1/paper.md"},{"t":"Backstage documentation","u":"https://backstage.io/docs/"}],"text":"A platform is a product with internal customers. Without measurement, you cannot tell whether it saves time or only adds a layer. What you will learn DORA metrics and what they show Developer experience: surveys and friction Platform adoption and time to first deploy Cost, reliability and toil measures Using metrics without gaming them DORA metrics Deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate and time to restore service measure delivery performance. The first two show speed and the last two show stability. Measure them per team from the delivery system and look at trends, not single numbers. Developer experience Quantitative data misses friction. Run a short survey each quarter and ask about build time, waiting, confusing tools and cognitive load. Pair it with interviews and observation. Track the same questions over time to see whether changes helped. Adoption Measure how many services use each golden path, the time from a new engineer start to first production deploy, and the time to create a new service. Low adoption is feedback about the product, not a failure of the teams that do not adopt. Cost, reliability and toil Track platform availability and incident counts, the support load in tickets and chat, and the share of engineering time spent on repetitive operational work. Show cost per service or per team so the savings of shared services are visible. Avoiding gaming Any metric used as a target will be gamed. Use metrics to find problems and start conversations, not to rank teams. Combine several measures, keep definitions stable and share results openly with the teams they describe. Measuring platforms: DORA and developer experience Flow in order: 1, DORA metrics to Developer survey; 2, Developer survey to Adoption data; 3, Adoption data to Platform roadmap. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. DORA metrics Observability What it does: Speed and stability. If it fails or is weak: Single number. Developer survey Observability What it does: Friction. If it fails or is weak: Not asked. Adoption data Observability What it does: Golden path use. If it fails or is weak: Low. Platform roadmap Step What it does: Acts on data. If it fails or is weak: Ignores it. Incident: A metric that rose while the work got worse Incident Deployment frequency was used as a target and teams split changes into tiny deploys. $ psql -h metrics -c \"select team, count(*) as deploys, avg(lines_changed) as avg_lines from deploys where month = &#x27;2026-10&#x27; group by team order by deploys desc limit 2\" team | deploys | avg_lines team-a | 612 | 3 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) When managers ranked teams by deployment frequency, one team deployed trivial changes to raise the count while lead time and failures did not improve. The platform team stopped ranking, reported the four measures together as trends, added the survey results and used them to choose the next improvement. Common mistakes Using DORA as a team ranking Measuring only speed Never asking developers Ignoring low adoption Changing metric definitions often"}});